    faq,
  )}
>
  <TopNav pageMode ctaHref={ctaHref} current="coverage" />
  <main>
    <section class="m-section m-page-hero" id="hero">
      <nav class="la-bc" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={hubHref} class="m-page-back">
          <span class="m-page-back__arrow" aria-hidden="true">←</span>IRS disaster relief
        </a>
        <span class="la-chip">P.L. 119-64 · signed Dec 26, 2025</span>
      </nav>
      <p class="m-eyebrow">Legal background</p>
      <h1 class="m-page-title">
        Disaster Related Extension of Deadlines Act: postponements now count for the refund
        lookback
      </h1>
      <p class="m-page-lead la-answer">
        Public Law 119-64 (H.R. 1491, signed December 26, 2025) amends IRC §7508A so a disaster
        postponement period is treated as an extension of time to file for the refund-lookback
        limit of §6511(b)(2)(A) — keeping payments made during the postponed window reachable by a
        later refund claim — and amends §6303(b) so collection notices account for postponement
        periods.
      </p>
      <p class="m-page-reviewed">
        Verified against congress.gov / govinfo as of <time datetime={reviewedOn}>{reviewedOn}</time>.
        Widely-repeated summaries dating the signing to January 2026 are wrong — the official
        record says December 26, 2025.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="m-section m-page-block" aria-labelledby="lb-trap-h">
      <h2 class="m-page-h2" id="lb-trap-h">The lookback trap it closes</h2>
      <p class="m-page-body la-body">
        A refund claim is limited by §6511(b)(2)(A) to the tax paid within the lookback window —
        three years plus any extension of time to file. A §7508A disaster postponement moved the
        filing deadline without being an "extension of time," so for taxpayers who filed on a
        postponed schedule, tax paid before the original deadline could fall outside the lookback
        by the time a refund claim was made. The Act closes that gap in the statute itself: its
        official title is to make "the postponement of certain deadlines by reason of disasters
        applicable to the limitation on credit or refund," and the operative text treats a
        disregarded period as an extension of time for filing the return.
      </p>
      <p class="la-sources">
        <span class="la-sources__k">Sources</span>
        <a class="la-link" href="https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1491" rel="noopener" target="_blank">H.R. 1491 — congress.gov ↗</a> ·
        <a class="la-link" href="https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/PLAW-119publ64/html/PLAW-119publ64.htm" rel="noopener" target="_blank">P.L. 119-64 statute text — govinfo ↗</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="m-section m-page-block" aria-labelledby="lb-notices-h">
      <h2 class="m-page-h2" id="lb-notices-h">The collection-notice change</h2>
      <p class="m-page-body la-body">
        The Act also amends §6303(b): the timing rules for notice-and-demand now take into account
        any period disregarded under §7508A. In practice, taxpayers in a postponement window are
        not supposed to receive collection notices computed as if the postponement did not exist —
        a mismatch practitioners saw regularly after large disasters.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="m-section m-page-block" aria-labelledby="lb-cpa-h">
      <h2 class="m-page-h2" id="lb-cpa-h">What CPAs should take from it</h2>
      <p class="m-page-body la-body">
        For refund claims involving any client who filed under a disaster postponement, the
        postponed period now extends the lookback — worth checking on amended returns where the
        original was filed late in a postponement window. And a collection notice that ignores an
        active postponement is now contrary to §6303(b) as amended, not just to IRS practice. The
        postponements themselves still arrive the same way — an IRS release with a code, an area,
        and a date; see the <a class="la-link" href="/irs-disaster-relief">current verified
        notices</a> and the companion
        <a class="la-link" href="/irs-disaster-relief/filing-relief-for-natural-disasters-act"
        >Filing Relief for Natural Disasters Act (P.L. 119-29) page</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="m-section m-page-block">
      <FaqList eyebrow="FAQ" title="P.L. 119-64 questions" items={faq} />
    </section>

    <section class="m-section m-page-block">
      <div class="m-page-cta">
        <div>
          <h2 class="m-page-cta__title">Deadlines move. The paper trail has to keep up.</h2>
          <p class="m-page-cta__body">
            DueDateHQ tracks every postponement against the clients it affects — with the relief
            code and source on each date, so what you deferred and why is always defensible later.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="m-page-cta__btns">
          <a class="m-btn m-btn--primary" href={ctaHref} data-event="marketing.legislation.cta"
            >Start free</a
          >
          <a class="m-btn m-btn--secondary" href={hubHref}>Current disaster relief</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</BaseLayout>

